hat the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $x\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\ge 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =1-y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.041,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.041}}≈1.021$\n\n- For $y=0.137,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.137}}≈1.076$\n\n- For $y=0.782,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.782}}≈2.142$"] ["question-b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d"] ["answer-a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/id #uuid "a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904"] ["answer-a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/value "1.021,\\,1.076,\\,2.142"] ["answer-992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359" :answer/id #uuid "992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359"] ["answer-992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359" :answer/value "1.443,\\,2.564,\\,3.671"] ["answer-23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998" :answer/id #uuid "23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998"] ["answer-23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998" :answer/value "1.546,\\,1.699,\\,2.335"] ["answer-856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2" :answer/id #uuid "856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2"] ["answer-856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2" :answer/value "1.940,\\,2.493,\\,3.058"] ["answer-0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d" :answer/id #uuid "0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d"] ["answer-0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d" :answer/value "2.633,\\,2.784,\\,3.093"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/correct "answer-a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/choices "answer-a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/choices "answer-992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/choices "answer-23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/choices "answer-856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2"] ["answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/choices "answer-0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d"] ["question-b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d"] [17592186188352 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b"] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/id #uuid "3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56"] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-147698"] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.209,\\,0.493,\\,0.826\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $1\\le x\\le e,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1\\le x\\le e$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ x & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.209,$ we get $x={e}^{0.209}≈1.232$\n\n- For $y=0.493,$ we get $x={e}^{0.493}≈1.637$\n\n- For $y=0.826,$ we get $x={e}^{0.826}≈2.284$"] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/id #uuid "6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49"] ["answer-632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d" :answer/id #uuid "632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d"] ["answer-632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d" :answer/value "1.232,\\,1.637,\\,2.284"] ["answer-e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025" :answer/id #uuid "e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025"] ["answer-e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025" :answer/value "1.343,\\,1.983,\\,2.487"] ["answer-9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288" :answer/id #uuid "9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288"] ["answer-9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288" :answer/value "1.398,\\,1.793,\\,1.942"] ["answer-f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa" :answer/id #uuid "f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa"] ["answer-f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa" :answer/value "1.602,\\,1.834,\\,2.673"] ["answer-ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d" :answer/id #uuid "ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d"] ["answer-ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d" :answer/value "1.920,\\,2.136,\\,2.536"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/correct "answer-632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/choices "answer-632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/choices "answer-e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/choices "answer-9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/choices "answer-f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa"] ["answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/choices "answer-ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d"] ["question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49"] [17592186188352 :knowledge-point/questions "question-3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56"] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/id #uuid "483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e"] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147745"] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.691,\\,0.741,\\,0.839\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0\\le x\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.691,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.691}≈0.831$\n\n- For $y=0.741,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.741}≈0.861$\n\n- For $y=0.839,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.839}≈0.916$"] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf"] ["answer-84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5" :answer/id #uuid "84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5"] ["answer-84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5" :answer/value "0.035,\\,0.351,\\,0.540"] ["answer-f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322" :answer/id #uuid "f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322"] ["answer-f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322" :answer/value "0.353,\\,0.494,\\,0.631"] ["answer-8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1" :answer/id #uuid "8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1"] ["answer-8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1" :answer/value "0.404,\\,0.646,\\,0.951"] ["answer-41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a" :answer/id #uuid "41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a"] ["answer-41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a" :answer/value "0.503,\\,0.725,\\,0.837"] ["answer-5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83" :answer/id #uuid "5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83"] ["answer-5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83" :answer/value "0.831,\\,0.861,\\,0.916"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/correct "answer-5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/choices "answer-84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/choices "answer-f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/choices "answer-8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/choices "answer-41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a"] ["answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/choices "answer-5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83"] ["question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf"] [17592186188352 :knowledge-point/questions "question-483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e"] [17592186189408 :tutorial/content "Most statistical computer packages have functionality that allows data from a particular probability distribution to be simulated. In this lesson, we'll learn how this process works.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a random variable with the cumulative distribution function \n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\,, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=F(x)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/f2/f26b4b7871d1f2b4c7a983986f596c4b599d24fbf34c87b443fb21025f26b5ad.png)\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to generate a sample of size $n=3$ from this distribution. To do this, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, draw a random sample of size $n=3$ from the uniform distribution $Y \\sim U[0,1].$\n\n- Then, find the values $x_i$ such that $y_i=F(x_i).$ In other words, \n\n$$\nx_i=F^{-1}(y_i), \\qquad i=1,2,3.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x)$ is strictly increasing and therefore $F^{-1}(y)$ exists for all $y\\in (0,1).$ \n\nSuppose that we run our simulation and generate $n=3$ numbers from $Y\\sim U[0,1],$ and that our three numbers (rounded to $3$ decimal places) are \n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.214, \\qquad y_2 = 0.707, \\qquad y_3 = 0.819.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll now use this data to generate three numbers $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3$ drawn from the distribution with CDF $F(x).$ The three $x_i$'s are the numbers shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d9/d93db6abed9b1f227e5badb0d71868b1aee261d803af929e1fd8e45dc5f2932b.png)\n\nTo compute our $x_i$'s, we first need to find the inverse of $F(x).$ So we first write $y=F(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y}-1 \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1-y}{y} \\\\ -x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1-y}{y})}^{-1} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{1-y}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 < y < 1.$\n\nFinally, substituting the values $y_1,y_2,y_3$ into the formula for $x=F^{-1}(y),$ we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1 \\approx -1.301, \\qquad x_2 \\approx 0.881, \\qquad x_3 \\approx 1.510.\n$$\n\nThe algorithm above can be formalized in the following theorem:\n\n*Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a \\lt x \\lt b,$ where $a$ and $b$ could be infinite. Then, the random variable $X,$ given by \n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\n is a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$*"] [17592186188461 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0]] "retractions" [[17592186188056 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.352, \\quad 0.567, \\quad 0.801$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ 2 - \\frac{2}{x},\\end{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le 2,; 1, \\mid x > 2$.\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186188056 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. To do this, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2 - \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\ny - 2 &= -\\frac{2}{x} \\\\\n2 - y &= \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{2 - y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.352$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.352} \\approx 1.214$\n- For $y = 0.567$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.567} \\approx 1.396$\n- For $y = 0.801$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.801} \\approx 1.668$"] [17592186188060 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.354, \\quad 0.593, \\quad 0.792$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n${\\cos x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186188060 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sin t\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\sin x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. So, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sin x \\\\\nx &= \\arcsin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.354$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.354) \\approx 0.362$\n- For $y = 0.593$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.593) \\approx 0.635$\n- For $y = 0.792$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.792) \\approx 0.914$"] [17592186189408 :tutorial/content "Most statistical computer packages have functionality that allows data from a particular probability distribution to be simulated. In this lesson, we'll learn how this process works.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a random variable with the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\,, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=F(x)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/f2/f26b4b7871d1f2b4c7a983986f596c4b599d24fbf34c87b443fb21025f26b5ad.png)\n\nSuppose we want to generate a sample of size $n=3$ from this distribution. To do this, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, draw a random sample of size $n=3$ from the uniform distribution $Y \\sim U[0,1]$.\n- Then, find the values $x_i$ such that $y_i=F(x_i)$. In other words,\n$x_i=F^{-1}(y_i), \\qquad i=1,2,3$.\n\nNote that $F(x)$ is strictly increasing and therefore $F^{-1}(y)$ exists for all $y\\in (0,1)$.\n\nSuppose that we run our simulation and generate $n=3$ numbers from $Y\\sim U[0,1]$, and that our three numbers (rounded to $3$ decimal places) are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.214, \\qquad y_2 = 0.707, \\qquad y_3 = 0.819\n$$\n\nWe'll now use this data to generate three numbers $x_1, x_2$, and $x_3$ drawn from the distribution with CDF $F(x)$. The three $x_i$'s are the numbers shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d9/d93db6abed9b1f227e5badb0d71868b1aee261d803af929e1fd8e45dc5f2932b.png)\n\nTo compute our $x_i$'s, we first need to find the inverse of $F(x)$. So we first write $y=F(x)$:\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + e^{-x} &= \\frac{1}{y} \\\\\ne^{-x} &= \\frac{1}{y} - 1 \\\\\ne^{-x} &= \\frac{1 - y}{y} \\\\\n- x &= \\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y}) \\\\\nx &= -\\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y}) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y})^{-1} \\\\\nx &= \\ln (\\frac{y}{1 - y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 < y < 1$.\n\nFinally, substituting the values $y_1,y_2,y_3$ into the formula for\n\n$$\nx=F^{-1}(y)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 \\approx -1.301, \\qquad x_2 \\approx 0.881, \\qquad x_3 \\approx 1.510\n$$\n\nThe algorithm above can be formalized in the following theorem:\n\n> *Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1$, and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a \\lt x \\lt b$, where $a$ and $b$ could be infinite. Then, the random variable $X$, given by $X = F^{-1}(Y)$ is a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.*"]]}]